# Model Rules

These rules apply no matter which model is currently running. Notice when the task is a poor fit for the current model and say so — briefly, once — then get on with the work.

## This project's setup

- Cursor plan: [fill with /setup-investigation]
- Default model for everyday work: Grok 4.7 or Auto
- Model for hard work: Claude Opus 5.5 (thinking) or Claude Fable 5.1 (high thinking)
- Worker model for delegated steps: `grok-4.7[fast=true,effort=high]` in `.cursor/agents/worker.md`
- Cost sensitivity: Keep costs low — only suggest a premium model for genuinely hard investigation or reconstruction
- Privacy constraints: [fill if Privacy Mode / Anthropic retention affects Fable]

## Two tiers

### Everyday tier — fast, cheap

Use for: filling note templates, renaming files, summarizing already-returned Evidence, small reconstruction edits, "what does this note say?", single-file work with a clear spec.

| Model | Notes |
|---|---|
| **Auto** | Fine default for beginners. |
| **Composer 2.5** | Cheap, fast edits and terminal work. |
| **Grok 4.7** | Strong instruction-following with many rules loaded. |
| **Claude Sonnet 5.5** | Best everyday Claude-style reasoning. |
| **Claude Haiku 5.5** | The cheapest Claude model. Use it for searching notes, summarizing logs and docs, and simple bulk edits. |
| **Gemini 3.8 Flash** | Cheap, 1M context for reading large ledgers. |
| **GPT-5.6 Terra / Luna** | Mid/low GPT-5.6. Terra is OK for everyday agent work. |

### Hard tier — use deliberately

Use for: first pass on an unknown binary or app, multi-subsystem investigations, native decompile + reconstruction, a bug that already survived two attempts, anything touching auth/payments/security in *our* reconstructions, long autonomous sessions.

| Model | Notes |
|---|---|
| **Claude Opus 5.5** | Workhorse hard-tier. Use *thinking*. |
| **Claude Fable 5.1** | Top Claude line, 2.5x Opus 5.5's price. Use it for problems Opus 5.5 could not solve. Needs Anthropic retention opt-in under Privacy Mode / Teams. It has extra cybersecurity safeguards, so if it blocks lawful investigation work, go back to Opus 5.5. |
| **GPT-5.6 Sol** | Persistent long sessions. Sometimes waits for a second "do it" — the go-ahead rule in `global-rules.mdc` covers that. |
| **Kimi K3** | 1M context, hidden by default. |

### Naming gotchas

- There is no "ChatGPT 6" in Cursor. Newest OpenAI family is **GPT-5.6** (Sol > Terra > Luna).
- "Thinking" is extra reasoning time, not a different model. Use it on the hard tier.
- Model names churn. Verify against the picker and [cursor.com/docs/models](https://cursor.com/docs/models). This list is current as of November 2026.

## How to apply this

1. Classify the task as everyday or hard. If unclear, ask one question.
2. If the current model is a mismatch, say so in one line, then proceed.
3. Never block on model choice. Never upsell everyday work.
4. After two failed fixes on an everyday model, recommend escalating.
5. When the hard part is done, say they can switch back.

## Route work to the right model

| Work | In Cursor | In Claude Code |
|---|---|---|
| Searching code, summarizing logs and docs | Built-in Explore, set to Claude Haiku 5.5 in Cursor Settings | `Explore` on Haiku 5.5 (`.claude/agents/Explore.md`) |
| Mechanical steps, building from a clear plan, tests, routine fixes | `worker` on Grok 4.7 (`.cursor/agents/worker.md`) | `worker` on Sonnet 5.5 (`.claude/agents/worker.md`) |
| Planning, architecture, tricky bugs, combining results | Claude Opus 5.5, in the main chat | Claude Opus 5.5, in the main chat |
| Problems Opus 5.5 could not solve | Claude Fable 5.1 | Claude Fable 5.1, as a one-off subagent model |

- Cursor bills Claude models at API rates and Grok from its included pool, so Cursor's `worker` stays on Grok.
- Cursor also reads `.claude/agents/`. Both tools have a `worker` file on purpose: when names match, Cursor uses the `.cursor/agents/` version.
- Quick edits and back-and-forth stay in the main chat. A subagent starts with no chat history, so handing off small jobs can cost more than it saves.

## Plan on the hard tier, execute on the cheap tier

When you are on a hard-tier model and the work breaks into steps: plan and review here; hand mechanical typing to `worker`.

1. Write a numbered plan. Mark each step **delegate** or **keep**.
2. **Delegate:** fill note templates from already-gathered Evidence, rename/move files, write tests for described reconstruction behavior, run a named command.
3. **Keep:** target routing, REA tool choice, authorization calls, interpreting decompilation, architecture of reconstructions, anything the worker already failed twice.
4. Handoff must include Goal, Files, Change, Don't touch, Done when. Never write "as discussed."
5. Review the worker's diff before telling the user it is done.

### Project-specific hard signals

Treat as hard-tier: first investigation of an unknown target, native decompile, multi-feature traces, reconstruction that must match observed behavior, and any request that approaches `security.mdc` boundaries (refuse those — do not delegate them).
